; LIMIT 05: a main that takes its input always exits 0.
;
; Costly, a little, in programs/json. A validator is the program the
; whole-input form of main is for, and it has to report invalid input
; with a nonzero status, which only the streaming form can return. So json
; declares the streaming main and reads standard input itself: a four-line
; read_stdin and a comment explaining why the obvious form was not used.
;
; No checker error: this program is valid, and does the wrong thing. It
; means "exit 1 on invalid input"; the int is printed instead:
;
;   $ printf 'x' | tools/l0 run programs/limits/05-whole-input-main-exits-0.l0
;   1
;   $ echo $?
;   0

(def main ((input (list int))) int
  (if (valid input) 0 1))

(def valid ((s (list int))) bool
  (if (is_empty s) true (if (is_digit (head s)) (valid (tail s)) false)))
